<%# THE CATEGORIES PAGE'S ONE CHANGE (spec §8.1). Local: `presenter` (CategoryBudgetPresenter).

    Four arms: §8.1's three — covered by an envelope, capped, neither — plus the account-pointed
    one the spec does not name and `Pool#return_holdings_to_the_account` creates in bulk. See
    CategoryBudgetPresenter's header for why that fourth arm exists and what was false without it.
    `Budget#category_must_not_have_pool` is what makes the pooled arms and the capped one mutually
    exclusive rather than an ordering choice made here — a category pointing at any pool cannot
    hold a cap, which is also why neither pooled arm renders an editor: there is nothing either
    could legally write.

    Rendered for every EXPENSE category now, where it used to render only for `budgetable?` ones.
    That widening IS the change: a category whose money moved into an envelope used to lose this
    block entirely and say nothing about where its spending now lives. %>
<div class="bg-white shadow-sm rounded overflow-hidden border border-gray-100 p-6"
     data-budget-block
     data-budget-state="<%= presenter.state %>">
  <% case presenter.state %>
  <% when :account_pointed %>
    <%# THE CATEGORY POINTS AT AN ACCOUNT, AND AN ACCOUNT IS THE BUFFER (§7.1).

        §8.1 does not name this state — it splits the world into "points at an envelope" and
        `budgetable?` ("no pool at all"), and this shape is in neither — but
        `Pool#return_holdings_to_the_account` produces it in bulk on every envelope destroy, so it
        is where a user lands the moment they un-envelope a category.

        NO STANDING AND NO ENVELOPE NAME HERE. Rendering the account's status would print the whole
        account's balance as this one category's, and calling it an envelope would promise a rule
        that `Budget#pool_must_not_be_an_account` forbids anything from ever writing.

        NO CAP EDITOR EITHER, and the sentence says why rather than leaving a missing button to be
        noticed: `Budget#category_must_not_have_pool` refuses a cap for a category pointing at ANY
        pool, an account included.

        THE REGISTER IS THE APP'S EXISTING ONE for this exact shape — `entries/_impact`'s honest
        card ("No envelope — this spending isn't budgeted. It comes out of your buffer. Give it an
        envelope on the Budget page") and `budget_page/_suggestion_rate`'s "currently comes out of
        your buffer", both of which speak about `Category#buffer_funded?`. Three screens, one
        sentence.

        THE HEADING IS `Pool#noun` THOUGH THE WORD DOES NOT CHANGE. An account's noun is already
        "buffer" — this arm is where the app's vocabulary was RIGHT — and reading it off the one
        mapping rather than restating it is what keeps the three types answerable from one place
        instead of three arms agreeing by coincidence. %>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-4"><%= presenter.pool.noun.capitalize %></h2>

    <p class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-900 mb-1" data-figure="headline">
      No envelope — this spending isn't budgeted.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
      It comes out of your buffer. Nothing reserves money for this category, and no cap can be set
      while it points at an account.
    </p>

    <dl class="divide-y divide-gray-200 mb-4">
      <div class="py-3 flex justify-between gap-4">
        <dt class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-500">Account</dt>
        <dd class="text-sm text-gray-900 font-medium" data-account-name><%= presenter.pool.name %></dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <%# THE POINTER WHERE THERE IS ONE, AND THE STANDING OFFER WHERE THERE IS NOT — one route to
        /budget either way, never both. `buffer_funded?` IS the rate detector's population, so this
        is precisely the shape the panel is most likely to be proposing an envelope for, and the
        pointer names what is waiting. Without a proposal the card would otherwise be a dead end,
        so it makes the offer in the impact card's own words. %>
    <% if presenter.suggested? %>
      <%= render "categories/_partials/show/suggestion_pointer", presenter: presenter %>
    <% else %>
      <%= link_to budget_page_path, class: "inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-medium text-brand hover:text-brand-dark" do %>
        <span>Give it an envelope on the Budget page</span>
        <%= heroicon "arrow-right", options: { class: "h-4 w-4" } %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  <% when :pool_covered %>
    <%# POOL-COVERED. No cap editor, by the model's own rule; the block's job is to say WHERE this
        category's money now lives and how that envelope is doing, in the app's one row vocabulary.

        A BUDGET POOL AND A SAVINGS POOL SHARE THIS ARM, and the line is the model's:
        `Budget#pool_must_not_be_an_account` lets a rule sit on either of them, `PoolStatus` gives
        both a row-vocabulary state (a savings pool's is `$424.00 of $2,400.00`), and
        `ENTRY_POOL_ID` resolves this category's entries into either the same way. Every clause of
        the sentence below is therefore true of both, and an expense category pointing at a savings
        goal is an ordinary shape on the demo — Health → Emergency Fund, Education → Retirement
        Supplement. An ACCOUNT is the one pool none of that is true of, and it took the arm above.

        `pool_status_label` with BOTH suffixes, exactly as Home's pools band and the Budget page's
        group header pass them — this block says how the envelope STANDS RIGHT NOW, and a screen
        that dropped a suffix would describe the same envelope differently from the two screens
        beside it on the same afternoon. `pool_balance_clause` puts the balance on screen for the
        three states whose figure is a bill's shortfall rather than the pool's money.

        THE NOUN IS `Pool#noun` AND NOT THE LITERAL "Envelope" (2d whole-plan review, fix 2). The
        arm is shared by a budget pool and a savings one, and it used to call both an envelope —
        while the pool card four inches below called the same savings pool a "Savings Pool" and the
        entry form's impact card called it a "goal". Three nouns for one pool, and the classifiers
        agreed all along: the words were the only thing that had drifted. `Pool::NOUNS` is now the
        single mapping all three read.

        The DOM hooks stay `data-envelope-*` on both arms. They name the block's slot rather than
        the pool's kind, the specs scope by them, and renaming them per pool type would give the
        page two hooks for one row. %>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-4"><%= presenter.pool.noun.capitalize %></h2>

    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
      This category's spending comes out of <%= presenter.pool.noun == "envelope" ? "an" : "a" %>
      <%= presenter.pool.noun %>, so it has no cap — the rule that fills the
      <%= presenter.pool.noun %> is what decides how much there is to spend.
    </p>

    <dl class="divide-y divide-gray-200 mb-4">
      <div class="py-3 flex justify-between gap-4">
        <dt class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-500"><%= presenter.pool.noun.capitalize %></dt>
        <dd class="text-sm text-gray-900 font-medium" data-envelope-name><%= presenter.pool.name %></dd>
      </div>

      <div class="py-3 flex justify-between gap-4">
        <dt class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-500">Standing</dt>
        <%# THROUGH `shared/_pool_status`, not hand-threaded (2d task 6 review). This block used to
            pass both suffixes itself, four inches above the pool card on the SAME PAGE describing
            the SAME envelope — two callers of one method, each free to drop a clause the other
            keeps, which is exactly the shape 2c's whole-plan review found between Home's two bands.
            `CategoryBudgetPresenter` already answered every question the partial asks, so the
            conversion cost nothing but this diff.

            The quiet tone comes from the partial now (`text-gray-700`, the row vocabulary's one
            grey) rather than this file's `text-gray-900`; the `<dd>` keeps the hook the specs
            scope by. %>
        <dd data-envelope-status>
    <%= render "shared/pool_status", row: presenter %>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <%= link_to budget_page_path, class: "w-full inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 rounded border border-gray-300 bg-white text-gray-700 text-sm font-medium hover:bg-gray-50 shadow-sm transition", data: { budget_link: true } do %>
      <%= heroicon "adjustments-horizontal", options: { class: "h-4 w-4" } %>
      <span>Rules on the Budget page</span>
    <% end %>
  <% when :capped %>
    <%# CAPPED — the existing editor, unchanged, plus the caveat §8.1 requires beside it.

        The sentence is `BudgetPageHelper#caps_not_counted_sentence`, the same string the structural
        check prints, because the spec asks for one spelling of it and because this is the screen a
        cap is WRITTEN on: a user setting one here and reading "$0.00 a period" on /budget an hour
        later has been told two different things about the same rule. %>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-4">Budget Management</h2>

    <dl class="divide-y divide-gray-200 mb-4">
      <div class="py-3 flex justify-between">
        <dt class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-500">Budget Amount</dt>
        <dd class="text-sm text-gray-900"><%= number_to_currency(presenter.cap.amount) %></dd>
      </div>

      <div class="py-3 flex justify-between">
        <dt class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-500">Prorated</dt>
        <dd class="text-sm text-gray-900">
          <%= presenter.cap.prorated? ? "Yes — tracks daily pace" : "No — flat monthly cap" %>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4" data-caps-note>
      <%= caps_not_counted_sentence %>
    </p>

    <%= link_to edit_budget_path(presenter.cap), class: "w-full inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 rounded border border-gray-300 bg-white text-gray-700 text-sm font-medium hover:bg-gray-50 shadow-sm transition" do %>
      <%= heroicon "pencil-square", options: { class: "h-4 w-4" } %>
      <span>Update Budget</span>
    <% end %>
  <% else %>
    <%# UNCAPPED — unchanged, except for the pointer §8.1 adds. %>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-4">Budget Management</h2>

    <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded p-6 text-center">
      <div class="mx-auto w-12 h-12 rounded-full bg-gray-200 flex items-center justify-center mb-3">
        <%= heroicon "calculator", options: { class: "h-6 w-6 text-gray-400" } %>
      </div>
      <h3 class="text-base font-medium text-gray-900 mb-1">No budget set</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">Setting a budget helps you track your spending</p>
      <%= link_to new_budget_path(category_id: presenter.category.id), class: "inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 rounded bg-brand text-white text-sm font-medium hover:bg-brand-dark shadow-sm transition" do %>
        <%= heroicon "plus", options: { class: "h-4 w-4" } %>
        <span>Create Budget</span>
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <% if presenter.suggested? %>
      <%= render "categories/_partials/show/suggestion_pointer", presenter: presenter %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</div>
